                {" "}
                <details className="block" data-ditto-id="style-span-2">
                  <summary className="list-item list-inside cursor-grabbing" data-ditto-id="style-link-31">
                    Review
                  </summary>
                  {" "}
                  <p className="block mb-4 text-justify">
                    I think my expectations of the book were wrong. I thought it was mainly going to be about magic, but it was more alternative history, politics and society, with magic on the side. Parts of it I really loved, and other parts were downright boring. I couldn't really care about Norrell or Strange, or any of their 'friends'. Norrell wants to bring back practical magic, and proceeds to hoard all magic books so that no one else can read them. I get that it's supposed to be a portrayal of academia, but to me that doesn't make an interesting read. I was already so sick and tired of Norrell halfway... Which brings me to my next point: no one was particularly likeable to me. I liked Stephen, Lady Pole and Childermass, and that was about it. After Lady Pole's disenchantment I hoped that we would finally get to know the real her but the ending just sort of fizzled out. After all that buildup I felt a bit betrayed. Also, I really missed strong female characters. All of them were wives, mothers or victims and mumbled about the same domestic things, the rest was mostly white men disagreeing with eachother.
                  </p>
                  {" "}
                  <p className="block mb-4 text-justify" data-ditto-id="style-button-3">
                    It frustrated me because the worldbuilding is so captivating, it's the only reason I was able to finish it. The family that was abducted to clean faerie pots, Lascelles who unwittingly takes the place of guardian of the Plucked Eye and Heart castle, the enchantment of Stephen and Lady Pole, the talking statues, the bells... those were all reasons I kept reading. I just didn't really like how any of the characters were written, they all seemed too oblivious or something. And so many things were left unanswered, about John Uskglass, Vinculus, Faerie, The King's Roads, and Childermass. It's like my mind was supposed to fill in the blanks, but there was more than enough room (1006 pages!) to dedicate to these things. I wish it had, because I loved those parts so much.
                  </p>
                  {" "}
                  <p className="block mb-4 text-justify" data-ditto-id="style-heading-8">
                    {"I still love Clarke's writing and worldbuilding, it's witty and funny. I just wish the book was ~300 pages shorter and contained more magic, less politics. I'm excited to read The Ladies of Grace Adieu and Other Stories, it's the same universe but under 300 pages and I hope it is a bit more of what I was looking for in JS&MN."}
                  </p>
                  {" "}
                </details>
                {" "}
